"""SEN0401 chapter 13, version 1.0.2: the teaching paragraphs of the sixteen worked examples with editable numbers. Every paragraph cites its saved source in the form of Stage 3.F."""
__version__ = "1.0.2"
E = {}
E["PinWaitByAttempts"] = (
 "A device that makes a thief wait longer after every wrong PIN turns a guessing game into a slow and finite one (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). The wiki describes waits of 1 second after the first failed attempt, 2 after the second and 4 after the third, and a wipe of the device after 16 failed attempts, so the waits before the sixteenth attempt are 1 + 2 + 4 + ... + 2 to the power 14, which is 2 to the power 15 minus 1, that is 32,767 seconds or 9.1 hours; in the example the number of waits can be changed, and every extra wait doubles the total. It is like a bank door that stays shut twice as long after each wrong code and finally calls the guard: the thief runs out of patience long before the door runs out of codes. The doubling is a property of the described rule; how long the real device waits depends on its firmware."
)
E["BrainwalletGuessTime"] = (
 "A secret that a person invents and memorizes can be searched as a list, while a random seed cannot (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026). The wiki says that such a brainwallet can be tested with dictionary attacks; as arithmetic, a phrase of 4 words from a list of 2,048 words, the list of BIP 39 (Palatinus et al., 2013), has 2 to the power 44, 17,592,186,044,416, possibilities, and at a rate of 10 to the power 12 guesses a second, an assumed figure and not a measurement, they are exhausted in 17.6 seconds, and every added word multiplies the work by 2,048. It is like a padlock with four dials of 2,048 digits each: a patient machine tries every setting, and only truly random choices make the number of settings the real number of guesses. The example lets the reader change the number of words and the assumed rate."
)
E["StretchingBuysDays"] = (
 "Wallet encryption slows a guesser down by repeating a hash many times, and the repetition count is the only cost that a short passphrase can be given (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The header of the crypter says that the default of 25,000 rounds is just under 0.1 seconds on a 1.86 GHz Pentium M, so at that rate a passphrase of 30 bits, 2 to the power 30 candidates, costs a single machine about 1,242.8 days to exhaust and every added bit doubles it; the example changes the thousands of rounds and the bits of the passphrase. It is like a revolving door that takes a tenth of a second to turn: no single push is slow, but a billion pushes are. The rate belongs to that one machine, so another machine changes the days but not the doubling per bit."
)
E["WordsFromEntropy"] = (
 "The length of a seed phrase follows from a small rule and not from taste (Palatinus et al., 2013). BIP 39 takes the first entropy-length-divided-by-32 bits of the SHA-256 hash as the checksum, appends it, and cuts the sum into groups of 11 bits, one word each, so 16 bytes, 128 bits, with a 4-bit checksum give 132 bits and 12 words, while 32 bytes give an 8-bit checksum and 24 words; the example changes the number of bytes and shows the checksum bits and the words. It is like writing a number on a form with a last digit that checks the others: the longer the number, the longer the check. The BIP allows entropy of 128 to 256 bits, so a byte count outside 16 to 32 is not a valid phrase, even though the arithmetic still runs."
)
E["TypoCaught"] = (
 "A checksum catches most typing mistakes but not all of them, and its power grows with the phrase (Palatinus et al., 2013). BIP 39 itself says that the checksum is short and that, with its 8 bits for 24 words, 1 in 256 random errors will be missed; the same arithmetic for 12 words, with a 4-bit checksum, lets 1 in 16, 0.0625, of random wrong phrases pass, and the example changes the number of words. It is like the last digit of a card number: it stops most slips of a finger, yet a wrong number that happens to fit still gets through. A checksum that passes therefore shows only that the phrase is probably typed right, and never that the wallet behind it holds the coins the owner expects."
)
E["BackupRangeCoverage"] = (
 "A backup knows only the addresses that existed when it was made, so coins sent beyond its range do not come back (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026). The help text of Bitcoin Core 31.1 warns that a smaller key pool may increase the risk of losing funds when restoring from an old backup and gives 1000 as default; on the test node with a key pool of 3 the stale backup covered the address numbers 0 to 2 and restored 1.0 of 3.0 coins, because 2.0 coins had gone to the tenth address, number 9 (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The example changes the pool size and the address numbers and counts what comes back. It is like a photocopy of a key ring: keys cut after the copy are missing from it. The numbers in the example are those of the recorded single run."
)
E["StuckChance"] = (
 "Several keys reduce the chance of losing access only if fewer than all of them are needed (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book says that multisignature addresses offer redundancy; as arithmetic, with 3 keys that are each lost with a chance of 10 percent, independently, an assumption and not a measurement, a 2-of-3 scheme is stuck when fewer than 2 keys survive, which has a chance of 0.028 against 0.1 for a single key, and the example changes the keys, the keys needed and the percentage. It is like a car with three keys of which any two are needed to start it: one lost key is a nuisance. Real keys are lost together when they sit in one drawer, so the independent chance is a lower bound and not a promise."
)
E["RobbedChance"] = (
 "The same redundancy has a price on the side of theft, because a thief needs fewer keys than the owner has (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). With 3 keys that are each stolen with a chance of 5 percent, independently, an assumption, a 2-of-3 scheme is robbed when 2 or more keys are stolen, a chance of 0.00725 against 0.05 for a single key, while a 1-of-3 scheme would be robbed at 0.14263; the example changes the keys, the keys needed and the percentage, so that the reader can see how the threshold moves the danger between loss and theft. It is like a safe deposit box with three locks: more locks that must all open protect against theft and more locks of which one suffices protect against loss, but never both."
)
E["MultisigVsizeFormula"] = (
 "Safety is paid for in bytes, and the bytes can be counted field by field (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book defines four weight units as one virtual byte; a one-input one-output spend has 82 bytes outside the witness, 328 units, and the witness of a 2-of-3 script holds the 2 marker bytes, the item count, an empty item for the old CHECKMULTISIG, 2 signatures of 73 bytes with their length bytes, the script length and the 105-byte script, 584 units in all, which is 146 virtual bytes and 1,460 satoshis at 10 satoshis per virtual byte, the same figure that the regtest transaction measured (Bitcoin Core, 2026). It is like a parcel whose postage depends on its weight: every signature and every key is a heavier parcel. The formula holds while the script length takes one byte, which is true up to seven keys, and a signature one byte shorter lowers the weight by one unit."
)
E["SmallShamirByHand"] = (
 "Secret sharing can be done by hand with a prime small enough to see (Shamir, 1979). The paper picks a prime bigger than both the secret and the number of holders and gives each holder the value of a random polynomial at that holder's number; with the prime 47, the secret 12 and the slope 30, the line 12 + 30x gives 42, 25 and 8 at x = 1, 2 and 3, and the first two shares recover the secret as 2 times 42 minus 25, which is 12 modulo 47, and the example lets the reader change the prime, the secret and the slope. It is like a banknote torn along a line so that any two pieces show the serial number. A prime as small as 47 is for teaching only: a real secret needs a prime far above it, as the 127-bit prime of the earlier example does."
)
E["OneShareFitsEverySecret"] = (
 "The strength of the scheme is that a single share says nothing about the secret (Shamir, 1979). The paper states that knowing fewer than k pieces leaves the secret completely undetermined; counting shows why for a prime of 11, because the share at x = 1 with the value 5 fits the 11 pairs of secret and slope (s, 5 - s), one for each of the 11 possible secrets, so a thief holding it still has all 11 to try, and the example changes the prime and the value of the share. It is like a sealed envelope with a number that could belong to any of eleven locks. This holds for a polynomial chosen at random with a prime far above the secret; a careless choice of the slope would leak."
)
E["ShareGroups"] = (
 "A threshold says exactly which groups of holders can open the secret and which cannot (Shamir, 1979). In a scheme that needs 3 of 5 shares, any group of 3, 4 or 5 holders can, which is 10 + 5 + 1 = 16 groups, and the 10 groups of 2 holders learn nothing at all, because k minus 1 pieces leave the secret completely undetermined; the example changes the number of holders and the threshold. It is like a board of five directors who need three signatures: any three can act, and two together have no more power than one. The count only describes which groups may open the secret and does not protect against the moment when the secret is put together again in one place (Wikipedia contributors, 2026)."
)
E["HeirWaitInTime"] = (
 "A relative wait can be written in blocks or in time, and the two are close but not equal (Friedenbach et al., 2015). BIP 68 counts a block-based wait in blocks and a time-based wait in units of 512 seconds, chosen because blocks come every 600 seconds on average; a wait of 10 is therefore about 100 minutes in blocks at 10 minutes a block and 85 minutes in units, and the example changes the number. It is like giving an heir a letter to open after ten rings of a bell, where one bell rings at the pace of the miners and the other by the clock. The heir's script with older 10 behaved as designed on the test chain, and the block-based form depends on the real pace of mining, which varies."
)
E["SequenceMaskArithmetic"] = (
 "A limit written in bits can silently turn a long wait into no wait (Friedenbach et al., 2015). BIP 68 says that a mask of 0x0000ffff must be applied to the sequence field, so the largest wait is 2 to the power 16 minus 1 = 65,535 blocks, about 455.1 days at 10 minutes a block, and 65,536 has none of its bits inside the mask and means a wait of 0; on the test chain the descriptor older(65536) was accepted and its heir could spend with one confirmation (Bitcoin Core, 2026). It is like an odometer that rolls over to zero after the last mark. The example changes the number of bits and shows the top value, the masked value of 65,536 and the days."
)
E["BitfinexHaircut"] = (
 "A custodian that is hacked can share the loss among all its customers, including those whose accounts were untouched (Wikipedia contributors, 2026). After the August 2016 breach Bitfinex reduced every account balance by 36 percent and paid back in BFX tokens in proportion to the losses, so an account of 40 bitcoin kept 25.6 and had 14.4 turned into tokens, and the example changes the balance and the percentage. It is like a bank that, after a robbery, takes a fixed slice from every deposit and gives vouchers instead. The percentage is the one that the article reports; what the tokens were worth later is not part of this arithmetic."
)
E["MtGoxShare"] = (
 "Numbers about a failure can be turned into a statement about the whole supply, and the statement shows how large the failure was (Wikipedia contributors, 2026). The article says that Mt. Gox reported the loss of almost 750,000 of its customers' bitcoins and around 100,000 of its own, around 7 percent of all bitcoins; 850,000 divided by 7 percent implies a supply of about 12.1 million coins at the time, and the example changes the percentage. It is like hearing that a vault lost a seventh of a nation's gold and working out how much gold the nation held. The figures differ by source, the Bitcoin Wiki speaks of 640 thousand (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026), so the implied supply is only as good as the quoted share."
)
